@h1 Embed Wit in an app h1@

Embedding Wit is the five-stage pipeline plus your own @code DataSource code@ —
no CLI, no config file, no subprocess. This page is the copy-paste starting
point: wrap the stages in one function, catch the two error families, and choose
fragment or document output.

@h2 The worked helper h2@

Verified end to end. Drop it into a server, a build step, or a static-site
generator:

@@@pre
import { parse, WitError } from '@witlang/parser';
import { loadExternalData, resolve, expand, RuntimeError } from '@witlang/runtime';
import { renderHtml } from '@witlang/render-html';

export function compileWit(
  src: string,
  file: string,
  data: Record<string, unknown> = {},
): string {
  try {
    const parsed   = parse(src, file);
    const loaded   = loadExternalData(parsed, data);        // dict DataSource
    const resolved = resolve(loaded, { rootPath: file });   // rootPath for `reference`
    const expanded = expand(resolved);
    return renderHtml(expanded, { mode: 'fragment' });
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof WitError || err instanceof RuntimeError) {
      const { line, col } = err.loc;
      throw new Error(`${file}:${line}:${col}: ${err.code}: ${err.message}`);
    }
    throw err;
  }
}
pre@@@

@code WitError code@ covers parse; @code RuntimeError code@ (and its subclasses
@code ResolverError code@ and @code ExpanderError code@) covers resolve and
expand. Both carry @code .code code@ and @code .loc code@, so one branch formats
them all.

@h2 The embedded data path h2@

The @code data code@ argument is a @code DataSource code@: a plain dict for
static data, or a function for dynamic. No config file, no subprocess:

@@@pre
compileWit(src, 'report.wit', {
  meta:  { version: '0.4.0' },
  sales: [ { site: 'Dunmore', lit: 'yes' } ],
});
pre@@@

Inside the document, @@@code @meta.version code@@@ and
@@@code (each @sales as s) code@@@ then read that data exactly as if it had been
written inline.

@h2 Cross-file documents h2@

If your documents use @code reference code@, pass @code rootPath code@ and a
synchronous @code fileReader code@ so the referenced files resolve against your
real or virtual filesystem:

@@@pre
resolve(loaded, {
  rootPath: '/content/main.wit',
  fileReader: (abs) => readFileFromCacheSync(abs),   // must be synchronous
});
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 @strong Build-time beats request-time. strong@ Prefer
compiling each @code .wit code@ once at build and shipping static HTML — the docs
site does exactly this. Request-time compilation is fine too, but cache the
result; the pipeline is pure, so the output is stable for a given input. aside@

@h2 Output choice h2@

@ul
@li @@@code renderHtml(expanded, { mode: 'fragment' }) code@@@ — an
@code <article> code@ to inject into a page you already own. li@
@li @@@code renderHtml(expanded, { mode: 'document' }) code@@@ — a standalone
@code <!doctype html> code@ file. li@
@li @code renderMarkdown(expanded) code@ — for a text or Markdown pipeline. li@
ul@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Forgetting @code rootPath code@ — a document with a @code reference code@
throws @code E_MISSING_REFERENCE_FILE code@ without it. li@
@li Using an @code async code@ @code fileReader code@ — it must be synchronous. li@
@li Catching only one error family — parse throws @code WitError code@, the
runtime throws @code RuntimeError code@; catch both. li@
@li Rendering per request without caching. li@
ul@

